cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use morphoGen in your work, please cite it as below, with the papers of the
  methods you use (docs/theory.md).
type: software
title: "morphoGen: morphological modelling and generation of virtual microstructures"
abstract: >-
  morphoGen generates random media from models of mathematical morphology and stochastic
  geometry, architected and bio-inspired materials, polycrystals textured after
  orientation maps, fibre and scale-rich networks and the microstructures grown by
  cellular automata on periodic and quasi-periodic tilings; measures them, their
  percolation thresholds and critical exponents included, against the theory of their
  models where it exists; estimates the size of their representative volume elements;
  meshes them from the exact functions of their phases; computes their apparent
  properties by finite elements on conforming meshes and by FFT on voxel images, on the
  CPU or an NVIDIA GPU: elasticity, transport and coupled problems, linear and nonlinear
  behaviours (von Mises, Hill, crystal and porous plasticity, hyperelasticity, MFront
  behaviours) in small and finite strains, fracture (phase field, cohesive zones, crack
  growth) and contact, and impacts by explicit dynamics with OpenRadioss; identifies
  their macroscopic law or learns one from their responses; and writes them for other
  codes (Z-set, MFront, AMITEX_FFTP, Abaqus, FEniCSx, Neper, DAMASK, OpenRadioss). A
  graphical interface, in the web browser or as a stand-alone application, chains these
  steps.
authors:
  - family-names: Dirrenberger
    given-names: Justin
    affiliation: "PIMM, Arts et Métiers, Cnam, CNRS UMR 8006, Paris, France"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3964-305X"
license: GPL-3.0-or-later
version: 0.6.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/jdirr/morphoGen"
keywords:  # those of pyproject.toml, and architected materials
  - microstructure
  - random media
  - mathematical morphology
  - representative volume element
  - homogenization
  - finite elements
  - FFT
  - Boolean model
  - Voronoi
  - TPMS
  - cellular automata
  - metamaterials
  - topology optimization
  - architected materials
